    "narrative": "The primary engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is **Corbin Carroll**, who sits at a tier-leading 85.94. His matchup against Tomoyuki Sugano is ideal, but the true value lies in the 87% stolen base probability triggered by the opposing pitcher's slow delivery, giving us a massive boost in our scarcest category. Joining him at the top of the board is **Tyler Soderstrom** (69.05) and **Seiya Suzuki** (66.75). Both benefit significantly from favorable matchups against high-xERA arms (Walker Buehler and Arrighetti, respectively), and despite some wind-in concerns for **Seiya Suzuki**, the combination of SP skill erosion and strong lineup placement makes them essential daily starts.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the optimizer today involved **Kyle Karros**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 43.54 score. While he presents a viable floor, he was sidelined in favor of the higher-upside profiles of **James Wood** (65.47) and **Mickey Moniak** (65.70), both of whom benefit from leadoff or near-leadoff placement, providing optimal opportunities for runs and stolen bases. **Zach McKinstry** also proved to be a value-play, surging to a 63.19 score thanks to a massive +16% park factor adjustment that outweighed his lower base projection. \n\nWe are monitoring lineup volatility closely for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Corbin Carroll**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Mickey Moniak**, as all rely on projected spots that have yet to be finalized by their respective clubs. While we aren't concerned about the underlying talent, any downward shift in the order for **Gabriel Moreno**\u2014currently batting 7th\u2014would further depress his score, though we are optimistic that **James Wood** at the top of the order will provide the necessary volume to keep our 5x5 totals healthy. Outside of our starters, **Josh Naylor** remains on the bench due to status uncertainty, leaving us to rely on our active core to maintain our efficiency marks.",
